-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/d9d688fe-be9b-496e-aff4-42c8e35fd102
-- title:
--   The four review-provision policies and the customers' information
-- statement:
--   A **review-provision policy** is one of four rules for what a second-period customer of cluster $n$ sees. The information is a $\sigma$-algebra on the sample space:
--
--   1. association-based AP: own-cluster reviews, generated by $y_n$;
--   2. global-based GP: the global average, generated by $\sum_l y_l=S_1\bar r$, the same for all clusters;
--   3. full-information FP: all ratings, generated by $(y_1,\dots,y_N)$;
--   4. joint-review JP: own-cluster and global reviews, generated by $(y_n,\sum_l y_l)$.
--
--   The sales vector is deterministic and public, so it adds nothing to these $\sigma$-algebras. Every one of them lies below the $\sigma$-algebra of the sample space (`info_le`).
-- source:
--   Xiao, Chen, Tang, Customer Review Provision Policies with Heterogeneous Cluster Preferences, SSRN 3403322 (manuscript of 2021-05-24), §3.3, pp.11–12; §5, pp.23–24

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ReviewProvision_ratingStat

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

set_option warn.classDefReducibility false

namespace ReviewProvision

/-- The four **review-provision policies**: association-based `AP` (p.11), global-based `GP` (pp.11–12),
full-information `FP` (p.23) and joint-review `JP` (p.23). SSRN 3403322 (manuscript of 2021-05-24). -/
inductive Policy
  | AP
  | GP
  | FP
  | JP
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- **The information of a second-period customer** of cluster `n` under a policy, as a σ-algebra.
SSRN 3403322 (manuscript of 2021-05-24), §3.3, pp.11–12 (AP, GP), §5, pp.23–24 (FP, JP):

* `AP`: own-cluster reviews `(θ k_n, r_n)`, i.e. `y_n`;
* `GP`: the global average `(θ ~k, r̄)`, i.e. `Σ_l y_l = S_1 r̄`, the same for all clusters;
* `FP`: all ratings `(θ ~k, ~r)`, i.e. the vector `(y_1, …, y_N)`;
* `JP`: own-cluster and global reviews `(θ ~k, r_n, r̄)`, i.e. the pair `(y_n, Σ_l y_l)`.

**Formalization Note.** `~k` is deterministic and public (p.11), so it adds nothing to the σ-algebra.
For the statistic `y` in place of `r`, see `ratingStat`. -/
noncomputable def info {N : ℕ} (p : Params) (k : Fin N → ℝ) : Policy → Fin N → MeasurableSpace (Sample N)
  | .AP, n => MeasurableSpace.comap (ratingStat p k n) inferInstance
  | .GP, _ => MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω => ∑ l, ratingStat p k l ω) inferInstance
  | .FP, _ => MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω l => ratingStat p k l ω) inferInstance
  | .JP, n =>
      MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω => (ratingStat p k n ω, ∑ l, ratingStat p k l ω)) inferInstance

/-- Every information σ-algebra is below the σ-algebra of the sample space, so the conditional
expectations of `posteriorMean` are not the junk value `0` of `condExp`. -/
lemma info_le {N : ℕ} (p : Params) (k : Fin N → ℝ) (ψ : Policy) (n : Fin N) :
    info p k ψ n ≤ (inferInstance : MeasurableSpace (Sample N)) := by
  have hsum : Measurable (fun ω : Sample N => ∑ l, ratingStat p k l ω) :=
    Finset.measurable_fun_sum _ fun l _ => measurable_ratingStat p k l
  cases ψ with
  | AP => exact (measurable_ratingStat p k n).comap_le
  | GP => exact hsum.comap_le
  | FP => exact (measurable_pi_lambda _ fun l => measurable_ratingStat p k l).comap_le
  | JP => exact ((measurable_ratingStat p k n).prodMk hsum).comap_le

end ReviewProvision


